 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# AND/OR simplification logic is broken for NULL values\n\nWhen using logical operators (AND, OR) with NULL values, the simplification logic in the optimizer is not handling NULL values correctly.\n\n## Description\n\nI noticed that when using NULL values in logical expressions, the optimizer is incorrectly simplifying them. For example, when using `NULL AND x` or `NULL OR x`, the result is not what would be expected according to SQL's three-valued logic.\n\nHere's a simple reproduction:\n\n```sql\n-- This should evaluate to NULL according to SQL's three-valued logic\nSELECT NULL AND TRUE;\n\n-- This should evaluate to NULL\nSELECT NULL OR FALSE;\n\n-- This should evaluate to TRUE\nSELECT NULL OR TRUE;\n```\n\nAfter the simplification, these expressions are incorrectly evaluated. The optimizer seems to be treating NULL values as FALSE in some contexts, which is not consistent with SQL's NULL handling rules.\n\nThis is particularly problematic when working with complex WHERE clauses that might contain NULL values, as it can lead to incorrect query results.\n\nI think the issue is in the simplify_connectors function where NULL values are being handled. The current implementation doesn't properly follow SQL's three-valued logic for NULL values in logical operations.\n</issue>\n",
